# Line endings and file types for CERNAL.
#
# Why this file exists at all:
# The Git for Windows installer sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide. Until this file
# existed, that setting won by default: every text file was stored LF in the repository
# but written CRLF into a Windows working copy. For most files that is harmless. For the
# `do` task runner it is fatal — a script whose shebang line ends in CRLF makes Linux read
# the interpreter name as "bash\r", and WSL answers:
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# which is exit code 127 and means nothing to anyone who has not seen it before.
# Git Bash happens to tolerate it; WSL does not. So the documented WSL setup was broken
# for exactly the people it was written for.
#
# A fresh `git clone` made after this file is committed already gets it right.
# An EXISTING Windows checkout does not: the files are stored LF but were already written
# to disk as CRLF, and Git will not rewrite them just because the rules changed. Check with
#     git ls-files --eol do
# If that prints `w/crlf`, commit or stash your work first, then force a re-checkout:
#     git rm --cached -r .
#     git reset --hard
# Anything that then shows as modified is a line-ending fix, not a content change.

# Let Git decide what is text and normalise it to LF in the repository. Text files still
# arrive in your working copy with whatever endings your platform and core.autocrlf want —
# this line only guarantees that what is *stored* is LF, so the repository never carries
# CRLF for one person and LF for another.
* text=auto

# --- Files that must be LF in the working copy too, not just in the repository ---------
# These carry a `#!` shebang and are executed directly (mode 100755). The kernel reads the
# shebang byte for byte, so a trailing \r becomes part of the interpreter's name.

do          text eol=lf
manage.py   text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never touch these, and never try to merge them ---------------------------
# Without this, an unlucky auto-detection could rewrite bytes inside an image or a PDF.
*.png       binary
*.pdf       binary

# --- Generated lockfiles ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Cosmetic only: this collapses them by default in GitHub pull-request diffs so a
# 30,000-line regenerated lock does not bury the twenty lines of science you actually
# want reviewed. They are still tracked, still diffable locally, and still must be
# regenerated with `uv lock` / `npm install` — never hand-edited, and never resolved by
# hand during a merge conflict.
uv.lock                     linguist-generated=true
frontend/package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
